VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing paraspinal muscle spasm rendering the patient unable to stand, ambulate, or find a comfortable resting position.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, severe exacerbation of unilateral back pain and muscle cramping following a prolonged period of standing, resulting in functional incapacitation and near-syncope from pain. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute intervertebral disc prolapse or spinal epidural pathology, and to administer potent parenteral muscle relaxants to break the severe mechanical pain cycle prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back suddenly locked up into a massive cramp, and now the pain is so sharp I can't even stand up straight or walk."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 4-hour history of acute, unremitting paraspinal muscle spasm on the right side of the thoracic spine. The episode was triggered by a prolonged 6-hour period of standing at a casual employment shift. The patient reports a chronic history of mild back ache and fatigue, but this acute, agonizing spasm is a novel exacerbation. The severe pain prevents any flexion or extension of the trunk. The patient notes an uneven waistline and a chronic prominence of one scapula, which they had previously ignored. The acute mechanical pain is entirely unrelieved by rest or standard over-the-counter analgesia.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; assuming a rigid lateral decubitus position; unable to ambulate without severe pain exacerbation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is highly distressed by the sudden loss of mobility and the intensity of the muscle spasm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely uncomfortable, diaphoretic, and extremely rigid, avoiding any truncal movement.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination of the lower extremities reveals normal tone, power (5/5), and reflexes, with an absence of severe neurological deficits, safely excluding acute radiculopathy or cauda equina syndrome. Musculoskeletal examination demonstrates a marked, board-like spasm of the right thoracic paraspinal muscles. Upon assisted standing, there is an asymmetric shoulder height and a visible lateral curvature of the spinal column. The Adams forward bend test elicits a pronounced right-sided rib hump deformity. A truncal shift resulting in an apparent leg length discrepancy is noted. The clinical presentation firmly satisfies the diagnostic tetrad of asymmetric shoulders, visible lateral spinal curve, and a positive Adams bend test, heavily anticipating a Cobb angle >10 degrees on future radiographs.
Environmental Observations: A heating pad is present but was applied ineffectively to the lower lumbar spine rather than the focal thoracic spasm.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Paracetamol 1000mg 2 hours ago with zero efficacy against the severe mechanical spasm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Paraspinal Muscle Spasm secondary to Undiagnosed Idiopathic Scoliosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of radicular pain shooting down the lower extremities, normal lower limb reflexes, and a negative straight leg raise test.

Renal Colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain being strictly mechanical, exacerbated by movement, and the complete lack of writhing behavior, haematuria, or costovertebral angle tenderness.

Spinal Epidural Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, no history of intravenous drug use, and a completely normal lower extremity neurological examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits an acute, severely incapacitating paraspinal muscle spasm driven by the chronic, asymmetrical biomechanical loading of an underlying, previously unmanaged scoliosis. The intensity of the pain and the resultant immobility necessitated an immediate after-hours home visit to definitively rule out an acute neurological catastrophe (such as a disc herniation or spinal cord compression). Clinical examination safely confirmed a purely musculoskeletal etiology while identifying the structural spinal deformity. Immediate parenteral intervention with a muscle relaxant was strictly necessary to bypass the GI tract, rapidly halt the severe muscle tetany, and restore basic functional mobility, a requirement that could not be delayed until the following morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Diazepam 5mg PO stat to provide immediate central skeletal muscle relaxation and break the acute spasm. Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat for potent, rapid-onset analgesia. Ordered non-urgent standing full-length posteroanterior and lateral spinal radiographs to formalize the scoliosis diagnosis and measure the Cobb angle.
Positive Behavioral Change: To actively break the mechanical pain cycle, apply a heat pack precisely to the asymmetric paraspinal muscle prominence for 20 minutes, directly linking the application of targeted heat to the cessation of the painful cramp. To prevent future acute spasms, strictly limit continuous standing to no more than 45 minutes, utilizing scheduled sitting breaks to directly offload the asymmetrical strain on the spinal musculature.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to review the spinal radiographs and arrange a referral to a physiotherapist for targeted core strengthening and biomechanical correction.

